  "profile": {
    "summary": "A south-east Bengaluru neighbourhood of eight blocks and one of the city's best-known startup districts, where offices, co-working spaces and cafés sit among older bungalows on tree-lined streets.",
    "character": "Koramangala was planned as a suburb after independence and stayed sparsely settled until the late 1970s. Its position between central Bengaluru and Electronic City drew new residents during the tech boom of the late 1990s. Today 80 Feet Road and the 5th Block streets around Jyoti Nivas College Road are busy with offices, co-working spaces, restaurants and pubs.",
    "connectivity": "The Inner Ring Road runs through the middle, separating Blocks 1 to 4 from Blocks 5 to 8, and Hosur Road runs along the western edge. Koramangala has no metro station of its own. The nearest are Suddaguntepalya (Tavarekere), the end of the Pink Line stretch that opened on 14 November 2026, and Central Silk Board on the Yellow Line, which opened in August 2025.",
    "goodToKnow": [
      "Low-lying parts of Koramangala flood in heavy rain. ST Bed Layout was still under water a day after storms in May 2025, and 4th Block lanes behind 80 Feet Road have had sewage-laden water enter homes in past monsoons.",
      "Pubs in 5th Block, many around Jyoti Nivas College Road, have drawn complaints from residents about loud music late at night.",
      "Junctions such as the Sony World signal are long-standing traffic bottlenecks, and Silk Board junction to the south also floods in heavy rain."
    ]
  },
